b/ref 1071 "By expressing $\\dfrac{1}{2}\\log_3{x}$ in the form $\\log_3 \\left(x^k\\right),$ determine the value of $k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110498 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "53a33ca5-8a37-5ec7-acb2-00e3471a1c6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110498 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n(c^m)= m\\log_n\\left( c \\right)\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡x & ={log}_{3}⁡({x}^{1/2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k = \\dfrac{1}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110499 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4080" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110499 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the value of $\\ln\\left(x^3\\right)$ if $\\ln(x) = 0.2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110499 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7e755fc4-40b5-5d64-8087-ae67baad204c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110499 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's recall the power rule for natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\ln\\left( c^m \\right) = m\\ln{c}\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms and the information given, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}) & =3\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110596 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110596 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110596 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110596 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110596 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110596 #edb/ref 1039 "Simplifying the Logarithm of a Power Function Using the Power Rule" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110596 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110596 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ddcd6d98-7aad-5d57-9a17-cc05cbce863a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110597 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111212 #edb/ref