7592186543525 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{5})=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543525 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "87e631c2-0787-575b-9e30-ac64f270159d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543525 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{5})=5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543528 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543528 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47230" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543528 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543528 #edb/ref 1071 "By expressing $\\operatorname{log}⁡\\sqrt{6x}$ in the form $p\\operatorname{log}⁡(6x),$ determine the value of $p.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543528 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8f6c580a-c90e-5ca5-ad86-b6a33b799e39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543528 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}⁡({c}^{m})=m\\operatorname{log}⁡(c)\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms, the given expression can be rewritten as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡\\sqrt{6x} & =\\operatorname{log}⁡((6x)^{1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{log}⁡(6x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $p=\\frac{1}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543537 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543537 #edb/ref 1069 "q-108205" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543537 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543537 #edb/ref 1071 "By expressing $\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ in the form $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{k}),$ determine the value of $k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543537 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9f684a9a-73a2-5a55-8105-9ed436326180" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543537 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3/4})\n$$\n\nHence $k=\\frac{3}{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543545 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543545 #edb/ref 1069 "q-100023" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543545 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543545 #edb/ref 1071 "If $\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)=5,$ what is $\\operatorname{log}⁡({x}^{6})?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543545 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "abb937a1-e6d0-5e25-8254-46eecc862782" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543545 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}⁡({c}^{m})=m\\operatorname{log}⁡(c)\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms and the information given, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡({x}^{6}) & =6\\cdot \\operatorname{log}⁡(x) \\\\  & =6\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543549 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543549 #edb/ref 1069 "q-100019" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543549 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543549 #edb/ref 1071 "$7{log}_{2}⁡t=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543549 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b071d322-d430-5f91-b157-0d44ee6fac29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543549 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}⁡({c}^{m})=m\\operatorname{log}⁡(c)\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n7{log}_{2}⁡t={log}_{2}⁡({t}^{7}).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
